    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting distinction. When you talk about \"rhetorical dominance\" or being \"inherently authoritative,\" it sounds like you're saying there's a specific type of personality or demeanor that just naturally fits those top roles. In your professional opinion, is that specific \"boardroom presence\" tied to certain cultural norms or ways of communicating that might make it harder for people from different backgrounds to be perceived as leaders, even if they have the same qualifications?",
